@page "/data-model"

<PageTitle>Data Model - OperationsFlow</PageTitle>

<div class="operations-dashboard data-model-page">
    <PageIntro Eyebrow="Technical Review"
               Title="Data Model"
               Subtitle="A technical and business overview of the main OperationsFlow records, fields, relationships, seeded demo data, and how those records feed workflow, reporting, workload, reminders, and data quality.">
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="technical-overview">Technical Overview</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="portfolio-hub">Portfolio Hub</a>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="data-quality">Data Quality</a>
    </PageIntro>

    <div class="page-purpose-note">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> use this page to explain how the project data fits together. OperationsFlow is built around business records with owners, statuses, priorities, dates, notes, and review rules so the same data can support operational screens, compliance review, management reporting, workload, reminders, and data quality checks.
    </div>

    <section class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">Model Style</span>
            <strong>Workflow</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">Records with owner/status/dates</span>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">Storage</span>
            <strong>SQLite</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">Local prototype database</span>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">ORM</span>
            <strong>EF Core</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">Models and persistence</span>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">Demo Data</span>
            <strong>Seeded</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">Realistic review examples</span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="summary-grid data-model-intro-grid">
        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Data Model Summary</h2>
                    <p>How the records are designed to support the workflow.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="data-model-copy">
                OperationsFlow uses business records that can be reviewed from multiple angles. A corrective action is not just
                a row in a table — it has a source, owner, priority, status, due date, completed date, notes, and activity history.
                That same record can appear in corrective action lists, safety meeting review, compliance calendar, workload,
                reminders, reports, activity log, and data quality checks.
            </p>

            <ul class="action-list">
                <li>Operational records hold the workflow detail.</li>
                <li>Shared fields make records reportable across modules.</li>
                <li>Management pages aggregate records by owner, priority, status, and due date.</li>
                <li>Data quality checks identify weak, missing, overdue, or expired records.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Shared Workflow Fields</h2>
                    <p>The fields that make records useful across the app.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="shared-field-grid">
                @foreach (var field in SharedFields)
                {
                    <article>
                        <span>@field.Label</span>
                        <strong>@field.Name</strong>
                        <p>@field.Description</p>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="data-entity-grid">
        @foreach (var entity in Entities)
        {
            <article class="data-entity-card">
                <div class="data-entity-card-header">
                    <span>@entity.Number</span>
                    <div>
                        <p class="eyebrow">@entity.Category</p>
                        <h2>@entity.Name</h2>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p>@entity.Description</p>

                <div class="entity-field-list">
                    @foreach (var field in entity.KeyFields)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <strong>@field.Name</strong>
                            <span>@field.Description</span>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>

                <div class="entity-used-by">
                    <strong>Used by</strong>
                    <span>@entity.UsedBy</span>
                </div>
            </article>
        }
    </section>

    <section class="report-card">
        <div class="panel-header">
            <div>
                <h2>Record Relationship Map</h2>
                <p>How records connect into the wider workflow and reporting model.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <table class="report-table data-relationship-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Record Type</th>
                    <th>Primary Purpose</th>
                    <th>Shared Fields</th>
                    <th>Feeds Into</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var item in RelationshipItems)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>@item.RecordType</strong></td>
                        <td>@item.PrimaryPurpose</td>
                        <td><span class="@item.BadgeClass">@item.SharedFields</span></td>
                        <td>@item.FeedsInto</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    <section class="summary-grid">
        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Data Flow Through The App</h2>
                    <p>How seeded records become useful review information.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="data-flow-timeline">
                @foreach (var step in DataFlowSteps)
                {
                    <article>
                        <span>@step.Number</span>
                        <div>
                            <strong>@step.Title</strong>
                            <p>@step.Description</p>
                        </div>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Database / Persistence Notes</h2>
                    <p>How this prototype treats storage and what production would change.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="database-note-grid">
                @foreach (var note in DatabaseNotes)
                {
                    <article>
                        <span>@note.Label</span>
                        <strong>@note.Title</strong>
                        <p>@note.Description</p>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-card">
        <div class="panel-header">
            <div>
                <h2>Data Quality Rules</h2>
                <p>Example rules that turn normal records into reviewable system-health issues.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <table class="report-table data-quality-rules-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Rule Area</th>
                    <th>Example Check</th>
                    <th>Why It Matters</th>
                    <th>Review Page</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var rule in DataQualityRules)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>@rule.Area</strong></td>
                        <td>@rule.Check</td>
                        <td>@rule.WhyItMatters</td>
                        <td><span class="@rule.BadgeClass">@rule.ReviewPage</span></td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    <section class="summary-grid">
        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Seeded Demo Data Strategy</h2>
                    <p>Why the sample data is intentionally realistic.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="seeded-data-list">
                @foreach (var item in SeededDataItems)
                {
                    <article>
                        <span>@item.Label</span>
                        <strong>@item.Title</strong>
                        <p>@item.Description</p>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Production Data Upgrade Path</h2>
                    <p>What would change for a real deployment.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <ol class="demo-step-list data-upgrade-list">
                <li>
                    <strong>Replace seeded demo data</strong>
                    <span>Use real company records, controlled imports, and user-created records.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Move to production storage</strong>
                    <span>Use SQL Server, Azure SQL, or another approved hosted database with backups.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Add user identity</strong>
                    <span>Connect created/updated/reviewed fields to authenticated users and roles.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Connect document storage</strong>
                    <span>Use SharePoint or controlled document libraries for real file links and review history.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Add validation and audit rules</strong>
                    <span>Enforce required fields, status transitions, review evidence, and activity logging.</span>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-card data-model-summary-card">
        <div class="panel-header">
            <div>
                <h2>Data Model Summary</h2>
                <p>A short explanation for technical or business reviewers.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="data-model-summary-copy">
            <p>
                OperationsFlow is built around workflow records that share common business fields: owner, status, priority,
                due date, completed date, notes, source, department, and activity history. This makes each record useful in
                more than one place. The same corrective action, document intake item, risk review, training record, or
                document review can support operational editing, safety meeting review, compliance planning, workload,
                reminders, reporting, activity traceability, and data quality checks.
            </p>

            <p>
                The current version uses local prototype data and realistic seeded records to demonstrate the workflow model.
                A production version would move to secure hosted storage, real user authentication, role-based permissions,
                controlled document links, stronger validation, audit rules, automated tests, backups, and monitoring.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

